for millions of his readers around the  world john le carre's work showed light  on a world of shadows in a writing  career spanning over  six decades likari authored 25 novels  and one memoir that came to define the  genre of the espionage thriller  it was art imitating life the first  three novels were written while likari  whose real name was david cornwell  was still working as a spy for the  british intelligence  his employers made him publish under a  pseudonym and he kept it for the  remainder of his literary career  titles such as the spy who came in from  the cold  tinker taylor soldier spy and the  honorable schoolboy  became cold war classics  likari's writings helped reshape  perceptions of that era  by portraying the treacherous antics and  moral ambiguity of the british and the  soviet spy agencies  after the end of the cold war he shifted  focus to the chaos of the us-dominated  world order  tackling issues from corporate  corruption and russian oligarchs  to extraordinary rendition and the wars  in the middle east  the themes are very relevant i think and  the the notion that we flood the world  with armies particularly the middle east  we never know where they'll end up we  don't know who the good guys are and the  bad guys  are good so i feel that i think that is  a theme we can  we can explore now as the news of his  passing was announced  tributes poured in from other genre  defining authors  on twitter horror and supernatural  fiction writer stephen king called  licare  a literary giant and a humanitarian  spirit  while the handmaid's tale author  margaret atwood said licari's novels are  key to understanding the mid-20th  century  in his last book published just last  year likari continued to draw on the  world around him  he brought the perfidy and deception  that's been central to his novels  into the world of brexit and donald  trump but in a life such as that of john  le carre  how much of it was fiction and how much  of it was true
 

